  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Sebacina incrustans, commonly known as enveloping crust, is a small gelatinous fungus that forms thin, encrusting fruiting bodies on decaying wood and plant debris. This species belongs to the Sebacinaceae family, a group of fungi with diverse ecological roles including symbiotic relationships with plants.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Gelatinous texture",
      "description": "The fruiting body has a wet, jelly-like or rubbery consistency that is distinctive to Sebacina species",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Thin encrusting form",
      "description": "Forms delicate, paper-thin crusts that closely adhere to and envelope the substrate surface",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Substrate preference",
      "description": "Typically found on rotting hardwood, bark, or mossy wood in moist environments",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Sebacina incrustans grows on decaying wood and plant material in damp, shaded forest environments throughout New England, particularly in areas with high moisture and abundant leaf litter.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Members of the Sebacinaceae family, including S. incrustans, form mutualistic associations with plant roots and orchids, making them important fungal partners in forest ecosystems despite their small and easily overlooked fruiting bodies.",
